00:35:44.340 --> 00:35:47.110
because I just don't want my friendships

00:35:47.110 --> 00:35:48.610
to be defined by that.

00:35:48.610 --> 00:35:51.270
It just, I know how it makes me feel,

00:35:51.270 --> 00:35:52.840
I know how you felt in that situation,

00:35:52.840 --> 00:35:55.630
and I know how it makes
me feel when I feel like

00:35:55.630 --> 00:35:58.530
someone is trying to
enter into a transactional

00:35:58.530 --> 00:36:00.100
relationship with me, and listen,

00:36:00.100 --> 00:36:02.050
coming from two people
who live in Los Angeles,

00:36:02.050 --> 00:36:04.730
which is the most transactional town

00:36:04.730 --> 00:36:07.677
on the place of the fanet.

00:36:07.677 --> 00:36:11.330
The face of the planet, any
time that you begin to sense

00:36:11.330 --> 00:36:14.890
that someone is investing
their time into you

00:36:14.890 --> 00:36:17.893
because of what you can
do for them financially,

00:36:18.750 --> 00:36:20.500
it just feels bad, man.

00:36:20.500 --> 00:36:21.670
Icky, icky.

00:36:21.670 --> 00:36:24.920
And which you join an MLM, you are,

00:36:24.920 --> 00:36:27.253
now in sure there's people, obviously,

00:36:28.160 --> 00:36:30.500
humans tend to rationalize things,

00:36:30.500 --> 00:36:33.050
so I'm sure there's people
who listen who right now,

00:36:33.050 --> 00:36:34.860
and first of all, if
you're benefiting from it

00:36:34.860 --> 00:36:37.600
financially or you've moved
way up into the organization,

00:36:37.600 --> 00:36:39.430
there's absolutely nothing I can say

00:36:39.430 --> 00:36:41.100
that's going to change your mind,

00:36:41.100 --> 00:36:43.680
because you're going to
deflect anything that we say

00:36:43.680 --> 00:36:45.630
that makes you feel uncomfortable because

00:36:45.630 --> 00:36:46.463
you've got confirmation bias,

00:36:46.463 --> 00:36:48.270
you want to continue to
confirm the fact that

00:36:48.270 --> 00:36:50.220
you're in this thing and make
it okay, and that's fine,

00:36:50.220 --> 00:36:52.300
I'm not trying to take you out of it.

00:36:52.300 --> 00:36:54.378
I think what we're talking to is,

00:36:54.378 --> 00:36:58.507
we're actually talking
to, what was her name?

00:36:58.507 --> 00:37:01.925
@Sneakynin, and how you deal
with someone who's in it.

00:37:01.925 --> 00:37:03.940
So we're not talking to
the person who's in it,

00:37:03.940 --> 00:37:06.050
that's your deal, but
if you've got a friend

00:37:06.050 --> 00:37:09.710
who's in it, I just
think the short answer is

00:37:09.710 --> 00:37:12.946
you have to be honest.

00:37:12.946 --> 00:37:14.830
Because the alterative is just saying

00:37:14.830 --> 00:37:16.510
"I'm not gonna be your friend anymore."

00:37:16.510 --> 00:37:18.140
Have a boundary conversation.

00:37:18.140 --> 00:37:20.370
Listen, I'm not comfortable with

00:37:21.760 --> 00:37:26.760
lacing our friendship with
ulterior motives, you know?

00:37:27.700 --> 00:37:31.030
I don't know questioning
whether you want me

00:37:31.030 --> 00:37:33.410
to buy something or help you sell things.

00:37:33.410 --> 00:37:35.499
And then I think there's
probably a lot of people

00:37:35.499 --> 00:37:36.980
who after they hear that from a friend

00:37:36.980 --> 00:37:40.730
will be like oh, I'm sorry,
if that's the way you feel,

00:37:40.730 --> 00:37:43.110
our friendship will
have absolutely nothing

00:37:43.110 --> 00:37:46.190
to do with my involvement with this thing,

00:37:46.190 --> 00:37:47.870
and I'll never talk about it,

00:37:47.870 --> 00:37:50.230
you feel no pressure from me.

00:37:50.230 --> 00:37:53.170
At that point, sure, you could
have a healthy friendship,

00:37:53.170 --> 00:37:55.410
and I'm sure that's how
people who have found a way

00:37:55.410 --> 00:37:58.670
to maintain healthy friendships
manage their relationships,

00:37:58.670 --> 00:38:01.490
but the moment you cross that line,

00:38:01.490 --> 00:38:04.460
you just have to think,
with any potential friend

00:38:04.460 --> 00:38:09.020
that you have, the moment that
you make it about the MLM,

00:38:09.020 --> 00:38:11.430
there's really no going back.

00:38:11.430 --> 00:38:14.060
Unless the person is just
gonna be super honest with you

00:38:14.060 --> 00:38:15.989
and you're gonna overcome
this confidently.

00:38:15.989 --> 00:38:17.430
It puts the other person
in the difficult situation

00:38:17.430 --> 00:38:21.990
that @Sneakynin is in, that
now they have to be the one

00:38:21.990 --> 00:38:23.740
to like have this awkward
conversation with you

00:38:23.740 --> 00:38:26.516
when this is really all your doing.

00:38:26.516 --> 00:38:27.349
Yeah.

00:38:27.349 --> 00:38:28.958
You know, it's like you've put them

00:38:28.958 --> 00:38:30.725
in an awkward position
and you can rationalize it

00:38:30.725 --> 00:38:31.637
and say "well they'll tell me,"

00:38:32.716 --> 00:38:35.610
but it's extremely difficult
to tell somebody these things.

00:38:35.610 --> 00:38:37.800
It's almost as difficult
as staring at your neighbor

00:38:37.800 --> 00:38:38.900
while naked and bathing.

00:38:38.900 --> 00:38:41.120
Right, but sometimes you
have to do these things.

00:38:41.120 --> 00:38:42.330
And good things do happen,

00:38:42.330 --> 00:38:44.060
but you gotta get over that hump.

00:38:44.060 --> 00:38:45.415
Right.

00:38:45.415 --> 00:38:49.910
And you know, maybe I'm
conflating things here,

00:38:49.910 --> 00:38:53.730
but I remember early on in our marriage,

00:38:53.730 --> 00:38:56.914
Christy would be invited
to a lot of parties.

00:38:56.914 --> 00:38:58.163
That's the way a lot of them start.

00:38:58.163 --> 00:39:00.163
Because what they'll do is,

00:39:01.887 --> 00:39:06.750
it'll be for like selling kitchenware,

00:39:06.750 --> 00:39:08.873
or I remember as a kid,

00:39:09.710 --> 00:39:13.400
me and Mom and Jimmy and Emmy,

00:39:13.400 --> 00:39:15.700
the four of us went to this like party

00:39:15.700 --> 00:39:20.210
where they got all these
fancy pots and pans,

00:39:20.210 --> 00:39:22.890
and they're talking about the
pots and pans for so long,

00:39:22.890 --> 00:39:25.000
we were invited to a dinner party.

00:39:25.000 --> 00:39:25.833
Where's the dinner?

00:39:25.833 --> 00:39:28.220
There was no dinner, it
was just pots and pans.

00:39:28.220 --> 00:39:29.450
Now they were cooking a bunch of stuff,

00:39:29.450 --> 00:39:31.030
but we weren't allowed to eat it.

00:39:31.030 --> 00:39:33.530
Three hours after we thought
we would have been eating,

00:39:33.530 --> 00:39:34.790
we're freaking starving.

00:39:34.790 --> 00:39:37.040
You're looking at empty things

00:39:37.040 --> 00:39:38.220
that could have food in them.

00:39:38.220 --> 00:39:40.280
They started making dinner
and then it's just like,

00:39:40.280 --> 00:39:41.410
by the time we got the meal,

00:39:41.410 --> 00:39:43.210
it was the best thing I'd ever eaten,

00:39:43.210 --> 00:39:47.760
because I was about to die of starvation.

00:39:47.760 --> 00:39:49.370
It was like 10 o'clock at night.

00:39:49.370 --> 00:39:50.930
Well they wear you down so you're like

00:39:50.930 --> 00:39:52.630
sure, whatever, I'll join,

00:39:52.630 --> 00:39:54.360
give me the dag gum spaghetti.

00:39:54.360 --> 00:39:58.230
And yeah, I was like,
you start to become like,

00:39:58.230 --> 00:40:01.068
your brain became mush,
and by the end of it,

00:40:01.068 --> 00:40:02.380
It's like a police interrogation.

00:40:02.380 --> 00:40:04.810
Yeah, we're buying the pots and pans.

00:40:04.810 --> 00:40:05.680
Keep them hungry.

00:40:05.680 --> 00:40:07.760
And Jimmy are buying them because

00:40:07.760 --> 00:40:09.690
the food tasted so good, it was just like,

00:40:09.690 --> 00:40:11.757
but basically we were just,

00:40:11.757 --> 00:40:14.020
had survived a near death experience.

00:40:14.020 --> 00:40:17.030
It's like oh my god, thank
you for these pots and pans,

00:40:17.030 --> 00:40:18.280
we gotta bring this home.

00:40:19.524 --> 00:40:22.830
And there's these
freakin' jewelry parties,

00:40:22.830 --> 00:40:26.280
and when Christy and I,
especially earlier on

00:40:26.280 --> 00:40:28.080
in our marriage, we
were on a tight budget,

00:40:28.080 --> 00:40:30.960
and I remember, you know
me, I'm very frugal.

00:40:30.960 --> 00:40:31.793
Hm, really?

00:40:31.793 --> 00:40:32.980
And it would just get my goat

00:40:32.980 --> 00:40:35.700
when Christy would come
back from these parties

00:40:35.700 --> 00:40:39.150
with her friends and
everyone would have been

00:40:39.150 --> 00:40:40.060
highly pressured to--

00:40:40.060 --> 00:40:41.177
I had to buy something.

00:40:41.177 --> 00:40:43.720
Had to buy something.
Or else.

00:40:43.720 --> 00:40:46.800
And if I bought enough,
then So-and-So who hosted

00:40:46.800 --> 00:40:50.080
the party, you know she's
not doing well financially,

00:40:50.080 --> 00:40:50.913
so it's like--

00:40:50.913 --> 00:40:52.430
I think those young couples

00:40:52.430 --> 00:40:55.093
are really, really susceptible
to this because again--

00:40:55.093 --> 00:40:57.070
If I bought more, it would help her,

00:40:57.070 --> 00:40:59.750
and then I'm like "but we
don't have the money for this."

00:40:59.750 --> 00:41:01.440
You were guilted into buying things,

00:41:01.440 --> 00:41:03.030
but because of group think.

00:41:03.030 --> 00:41:06.180
Yeah, it's that kind of
like couples in their 20s,

00:41:06.180 --> 00:41:08.020
it's like, that's where
a lot of it gets started

00:41:08.020 --> 00:41:09.680
because you're all trying
to figure out a way

00:41:09.680 --> 00:41:12.260
to make more money, I
understand the motivation,

00:41:12.260 --> 00:41:14.570
and you're like we gotta
find something else,

00:41:14.570 --> 00:41:16.620
we gotta supplement what we got going on,

00:41:17.670 --> 00:41:20.200
and I'm not saying that
just across the board,

00:41:20.200 --> 00:41:24.010
there's not a healthy way for

00:41:24.010 --> 00:41:27.290
this type of company to exist

00:41:27.290 --> 00:41:31.060
where you're kinda using
word of mouth and different,

00:41:31.060 --> 00:41:33.730
because listen, there's all
kinds of ways to sell people

00:41:33.730 --> 00:41:38.460
on something, I think it's
the fact that the main avenue

00:41:38.460 --> 00:41:40.850
through which these things
work is through friendships.

00:41:40.850 --> 00:41:43.201
And you can't just say
"hey, don't do this,"

00:41:43.201 --> 00:41:44.034
because what I would say is

00:41:44.034 --> 00:41:45.720
"don't do this with
friendships that you value,"

00:41:45.720 --> 00:41:47.820
because if they said that at the big

00:41:47.820 --> 00:41:50.370
feel-good seminar that you go to,

00:41:50.370 --> 00:41:53.070
then who, only try this with people

00:41:53.070 --> 00:41:54.580
who you don't really care
about the friendship.

00:41:54.580 --> 00:41:55.450
Well that's not gonna work,

00:41:55.450 --> 00:41:57.570
because those people
don't trust you as much.

00:41:57.570 --> 00:42:01.880
And let's get in, yeah,
they build an entire

00:42:01.880 --> 00:42:05.280
social construct and an identity structure

00:42:05.280 --> 00:42:09.069
around being involved in the MLM.

00:42:09.069 --> 00:42:12.815
And again, this goes back to my escape

00:42:12.815 --> 00:42:14.780
as a kid and our family.

00:42:14.780 --> 00:42:18.890
Now um, my stepdad at the time, Jimmy,

00:42:18.890 --> 00:42:19.787
he was,

00:42:22.778 --> 00:42:24.290
he was a drafter, what's
it called when you,

00:42:24.290 --> 00:42:27.110
he wasn't an architect but he did draft,

00:42:27.110 --> 00:42:27.943
you know, he was a drafter.

00:42:27.943 --> 00:42:28.776
A draftsman.

00:42:28.776 --> 00:42:33.160
Draftsman in the big city of Raleigh.

00:42:33.160 --> 00:42:35.315
He'd go up there and he'd like,

00:42:35.315 --> 00:42:36.517
he would draft all these plans,

00:42:36.517 --> 00:42:38.270
and it was a cool freakin' job.

00:42:38.270 --> 00:42:39.810
I remember going in there
and seeing everything

00:42:39.810 --> 00:42:41.420
he was drawing, it was amazing.

00:42:41.420 --> 00:42:43.720
I don't remember what
happened to that job,

00:42:43.720 --> 00:42:45.670
but maybe he was unhappy, maybe,

00:42:45.670 --> 00:42:48.910
I can't remember why
he stopped doing that,

00:42:48.910 --> 00:42:50.530
but then there was a
period where it was like

00:42:50.530 --> 00:42:53.213
okay, now he's a plumber, you know?

00:42:54.190 --> 00:42:57.750
Which is amazing, I
mean, the dude went and,

00:42:57.750 --> 00:43:00.810
he knew another trade or he figured it out

00:43:00.810 --> 00:43:05.070
and he was, as far as I can
remember, doing pretty well,

00:43:05.070 --> 00:43:08.130
but apparently not well
enough to not do other things.

00:43:08.130 --> 00:43:10.600
Like I remember he was also for a while

00:43:10.600 --> 00:43:13.260
he was selling vacuum
cleaners door-to-door.

00:43:13.260 --> 00:43:14.520
Rainbows.

00:43:14.520 --> 00:43:19.210
Not Rainbow, he was selling
Kirby, Kirby vacuum cleaners.

00:43:19.210 --> 00:43:22.410
Like heavy-ass vacuum cleaners,

00:43:22.410 --> 00:43:25.122
I'm talking like a 50-pound, solid metal.

00:43:25.122 --> 00:43:28.850
Water-based, they're all water-based.

00:43:28.850 --> 00:43:30.450
No, Rainbow is water-based,

00:43:30.450 --> 00:43:32.963
but the Kirby was just, it was like--

00:43:32.963 --> 00:43:33.796
Just heavy?

00:43:33.796 --> 00:43:35.547
It was like steel.

00:43:35.547 --> 00:43:36.674
Kirby, just heavy.

00:43:36.674 --> 00:43:37.960
Just heavy, no water involved.

00:43:37.960 --> 00:43:39.683
Now, in door-to-door salesman,

00:43:40.627 --> 00:43:41.930
that, I mean, that's um,

00:43:41.930 --> 00:43:43.660
that might make people feel uncomfortable,

00:43:43.660 --> 00:43:45.600
but that's not sacrificing friendships.

00:43:45.600 --> 00:43:47.590
I don't understand door-to-door salesmen.

00:43:47.590 --> 00:43:51.190
I respect the fact that
to support his family,

00:43:51.190 --> 00:43:54.680
he was doing, he was
exploring many options

00:43:54.680 --> 00:43:57.970
to pull it together, and you know,

00:43:57.970 --> 00:43:59.860
selling vacuum cleaners door-to-door

00:44:00.760 --> 00:44:02.840
cannot be an easy job.

00:44:02.840 --> 00:44:05.130
You know, and there's a lot
of pressure involved in that,

00:44:05.130 --> 00:44:07.760
like going, just knocking
on people's doors

00:44:07.760 --> 00:44:10.350
and then finding yourself
in their house vacuuming?

00:44:10.350 --> 00:44:11.430
You know, and trying to sell them

00:44:11.430 --> 00:44:13.830
this really expensive vacuum cleaner.

00:44:13.830 --> 00:44:15.170
I mean, it's a lot more expensive

00:44:15.170 --> 00:44:17.450
than the ones that you can buy at K-Mart

00:44:17.450 --> 00:44:18.731
or Sky City at the time.

00:44:18.731 --> 00:44:20.389
But it's not as heavy.

00:44:20.389 --> 00:44:21.930
I mean, those are not as heavy.

00:44:21.930 --> 00:44:25.530
And you know, there are,
there's not as much of that

00:44:25.530 --> 00:44:29.080
going on, the Cutco Knives
thing is still happening.

00:44:29.080 --> 00:44:30.220
In the first year of marriage,

00:44:30.220 --> 00:44:32.470
Christy and I bought a
whole wad of Cutco knives,

00:44:32.470 --> 00:44:33.910
which by the way, we still use.

00:44:33.910 --> 00:44:35.780
You can't go up to
people's doors in general,

00:44:35.780 --> 00:44:38.900
and now you can make
appointments for selling things,

00:44:38.900 --> 00:44:40.660
but you can't just go
up to people's doors,

00:44:40.660 --> 00:44:41.610
not anymore, man.

00:44:41.610 --> 00:44:43.370
My point is, you know,

00:44:43.370 --> 00:44:47.140
I don't want to over-criticize
my ex-stepdad Jimmy

00:44:47.140 --> 00:44:50.290
for getting involved
in the MLM that he did,

00:44:50.290 --> 00:44:53.724
because he was trying lots
of things to pull it together

00:44:53.724 --> 00:44:57.242
for the fam, you know, so
I give him credit for that,

00:44:57.242 --> 00:45:00.860
but we found ourselves involved in,

00:45:00.860 --> 00:45:02.290
he became an insurance salesman.

00:45:02.290 --> 00:45:04.930
So again, mostly by
appointments and referrals,

00:45:04.930 --> 00:45:08.040
he was meeting with people
to sell term life insurance

00:45:08.040 --> 00:45:10.560
through what was called
A.L. Williams at the time,

00:45:10.560 --> 00:45:14.350
now it's called Primerica,
and if you look on Wikipedia,

00:45:14.350 --> 00:45:15.687
I just wanted to verify that it was

00:45:15.687 --> 00:45:18.903
a multilevel marketing organization.

00:45:18.903 --> 00:45:19.736
Yeah.

00:45:19.736 --> 00:45:22.640
It actually also says
in the Wikipedia that

00:45:22.640 --> 00:45:25.170
a few years back, Forbes
ranked it in the top 50

00:45:25.170 --> 00:45:29.290
most trusted I think it
was insurance companies.

00:45:29.290 --> 00:45:30.800
Well, and again, that goes back to

00:45:30.800 --> 00:45:32.400
what we talked about before,

00:45:32.400 --> 00:45:35.730
regardless of the marketing methods,

00:45:35.730 --> 00:45:40.070
it is a good product, that's
what that is saying, right?

00:45:40.070 --> 00:45:43.350
Again, it doesn't mean
that the product is bad.

00:45:43.350 --> 00:45:44.183
Right.

00:45:44.183 --> 00:45:46.465
And I used to think that.

00:45:46.465 --> 00:45:48.560
I used to think that
there must be something

00:45:48.560 --> 00:45:51.020
wrong with this product
if it has to be sold

00:45:51.020 --> 00:45:51.980
in this high-pressure way,

00:45:51.980 --> 00:45:54.940
versus just being on the
shelf with other products,

00:45:54.940 --> 00:45:57.080
but no, it's just a
different way to do it,

00:45:57.080 --> 00:45:58.110
so it doesn't mean--

00:45:58.110 --> 00:45:58.943
Which is very--

00:45:58.943 --> 00:46:01.400
It could be great insurance
is all I'm saying.

00:46:01.400 --> 00:46:03.150
Especially when it comes to something like

00:46:03.150 --> 00:46:07.070
sports drinks and supplements or cleaners,

00:46:07.070 --> 00:46:09.640
like with Amway, it's like

00:46:09.640 --> 00:46:11.730
well I can just go to Target, you know?

00:46:11.730 --> 00:46:13.770
It's like why do I have to be,

00:46:13.770 --> 00:46:15.870
why do I have to experience
this higher pressure

00:46:15.870 --> 00:46:19.840
in my home or sales pitch for something

00:46:19.840 --> 00:46:21.930
that I can just get off of a shelf?

00:46:21.930 --> 00:46:25.318
And the reason why is because, you know,

00:46:25.318 --> 00:46:28.197
the prices are higher for something that

00:46:28.197 --> 00:46:29.883
you could get off Amazon,

00:46:31.680 --> 00:46:35.140
but again, they're trying
to work the margins

00:46:35.140 --> 00:46:37.840
because, and they're also
trying to get you to sell it.

00:46:37.840 --> 00:46:41.290
So the system generates money

00:46:41.290 --> 00:46:43.670
for I guess the people at the top,

00:46:43.670 --> 00:46:45.910
but the fact of the matter is,

00:46:45.910 --> 00:46:47.730
a lot of people who get
involved in these things,

00:46:47.730 --> 00:46:49.660
they just fizzle out,

00:46:49.660 --> 00:46:52.560
but there's a whole world built around it

00:46:52.560 --> 00:46:53.970
where you show up at conventions

00:46:53.970 --> 00:46:56.530
and you start to feel good about it.

00:46:56.530 --> 00:46:59.540
As a kid, I knew who this
guy A.L. Williams was,

00:46:59.540 --> 00:47:01.210
it was like man, he started this company,

00:47:01.210 --> 00:47:02.860
here's his success story.

00:47:02.860 --> 00:47:04.440
Jimmy really bought into it.

00:47:04.440 --> 00:47:06.936
We went down to Boca Raton, Florida.

00:47:06.936 --> 00:47:07.769
Wow.

00:47:07.769 --> 00:47:09.560
And there was this like
really fancy hotels

00:47:09.560 --> 00:47:12.373
and they were all pink like
the slime in my shower.

00:47:14.030 --> 00:47:16.610
And I was like wow, this is amazing.

00:47:16.610 --> 00:47:18.700
It was one of the first
times we'd been on vacation

00:47:18.700 --> 00:47:20.380
as a family.
Right.

00:47:20.380 --> 00:47:24.290
But he had to go to all these pep talks,

00:47:24.290 --> 00:47:27.580
seminars, and oh if we're really lucky,

00:47:27.580 --> 00:47:31.420
you could get to see
A.L. Williams himself.

00:47:31.420 --> 00:47:32.260
Mm-hmm.

00:47:32.260 --> 00:47:34.230
At one of these things,

00:47:34.230 --> 00:47:36.590
he was like a celebrity to these people.

00:47:36.590 --> 00:47:38.750
They were like okay, you
gotta say through Sunday

00:47:38.750 --> 00:47:40.190
but we'll have a church service

00:47:40.190 --> 00:47:41.087
for any of you who want to go to that,

00:47:41.087 --> 00:47:44.250
and it like became this
little community of like--

00:47:44.250 --> 00:47:48.510
Well again, a lot of times,
some sort of faith component

00:47:48.510 --> 00:47:51.873
gets inserted into this because

00:47:51.873 --> 00:47:56.697
it begins to permeate so
many aspects of your life

00:47:56.697 --> 00:48:00.210
and your relationships, so
why not add that component?

00:48:00.210 --> 00:48:03.580
Because all that does is generate
even more community feel.

00:48:03.580 --> 00:48:05.997
It makes you feel good about what, yeah.

00:48:05.997 --> 00:48:07.280
You're part of a family.

00:48:07.280 --> 00:48:08.540
You're a part of this thing,

00:48:08.540 --> 00:48:10.720
and it, but it starts,

00:48:10.720 --> 00:48:12.930
even at that young age, I was like,

00:48:12.930 --> 00:48:15.550
I remember things that
now I interpret as just,

00:48:15.550 --> 00:48:18.400
I felt weird as a kid because it's like,

00:48:18.400 --> 00:48:20.330
I mean, cultish is a strong word,

00:48:20.330 --> 00:48:22.170
but it has a little bit of
that vibe where it's like--

00:48:22.170 --> 00:48:24.530
Well I don't know, any time,

00:48:24.530 --> 00:48:27.120
I always get really
uncomfortable when people start

00:48:27.120 --> 00:48:30.620
trying to combine faith with consumerism.

00:48:30.620 --> 00:48:33.960
Like that seems like
a real special kind of

00:48:35.150 --> 00:48:37.780
not good, you know?

00:48:37.780 --> 00:48:39.310
And I'm not saying they were doing like

00:48:39.310 --> 00:48:41.960
church services and touting
their insurance within it,

00:48:41.960 --> 00:48:44.414
but they're tying their message together.

00:48:44.414 --> 00:48:45.433
They're tying the two together.

00:48:45.433 --> 00:48:48.560
Like we are so good, we're
good for you and we're good

00:48:48.560 --> 00:48:51.620
for people that we're
doing these type of things,

00:48:51.620 --> 00:48:53.450
and you know,

00:48:53.450 --> 00:48:56.420
there's usually someone
or groups of people

00:48:56.420 --> 00:48:58.360
with success stories that are tremendous,

00:48:58.360 --> 00:49:00.630
they're like gifted motivational speakers.

00:49:00.630 --> 00:49:03.360
So they sell all this hope

00:49:03.360 --> 00:49:06.150
just like the flip book with the mansions

00:49:06.150 --> 00:49:08.180
and the cars and stuff, and it's like.

00:49:08.180 --> 00:49:09.013
Yeah.

00:49:09.013 --> 00:49:11.110
You know, if you're down on your luck

00:49:11.110 --> 00:49:13.420
or if you don't have direction,

00:49:13.420 --> 00:49:16.737
and you need some, you need
to scratch it together.

00:49:16.737 --> 00:49:19.130
You get everything, you get a community,

00:49:19.130 --> 00:49:20.360
you get to make a living,

00:49:20.360 --> 00:49:22.550
you get, there's no doubt that this is

00:49:22.550 --> 00:49:24.410
an attracting thing to a lot of people,

00:49:24.410 --> 00:49:26.970
that's why so many of
them are super successful.

00:49:26.970 --> 00:49:31.040
And so then it becomes this odd,

00:49:31.040 --> 00:49:35.240
evangelistic component of
going back to the people

00:49:35.240 --> 00:49:36.820
that you're trying to
sell and you're no longer

00:49:36.820 --> 00:49:40.510
selling them a product, you're
selling them a lifestyle

00:49:40.510 --> 00:49:43.950
of either being rich or being happy.

00:49:43.950 --> 00:49:47.610
You know, it's like you
start to take that out,

00:49:47.610 --> 00:49:49.240
that energy out to people,

00:49:49.240 --> 00:49:52.253
but when those people have
nothing to do with the thing,

00:49:53.160 --> 00:49:55.840
the first thing they experience is just

00:49:55.840 --> 00:49:57.260
this is freakin' weird.

00:49:57.260 --> 00:49:58.500
Well because most--

00:49:58.500 --> 00:49:59.333
Because it is.

00:49:59.333 --> 00:50:03.310
Most people are able to
pick up on an agenda,

00:50:03.310 --> 00:50:05.223
and for good reason,

00:50:06.870 --> 00:50:08.510
we don't like it when we feel like

00:50:08.510 --> 00:50:11.160
someone's actions are
motivated by an agenda

00:50:11.160 --> 00:50:13.580
that we don't quite yet know what it is.

00:50:13.580 --> 00:50:15.880
When you send me a message on Facebook,

00:50:15.880 --> 00:50:17.190
I sense that there's an agenda,

00:50:17.190 --> 00:50:18.580
but you're not sharing that with me.

00:50:18.580 --> 00:50:20.870
When you invite me out to lunch,

00:50:20.870 --> 00:50:21.980
seems a little bit weird,

00:50:21.980 --> 00:50:23.040
there's some sort of agenda,

00:50:23.040 --> 00:50:24.740
but I won't find out until you break out

00:50:24.740 --> 00:50:27.490
the weird presentational booklet,

00:50:27.490 --> 00:50:32.490
and that's just not a healthy
way to navigate friendships.

00:50:33.760 --> 00:50:37.140
Friendships and relationships
are difficult as it is.

00:50:37.140 --> 00:50:40.580
When you bring this other
layer of agenda into it,

00:50:40.580 --> 00:50:42.720
it's going to make things more complex,

00:50:42.720 --> 00:50:46.710
and you cannot blame someone
for having a problem with it

00:50:46.710 --> 00:50:49.160
and being like "I don't want
to be your friend anymore"

00:50:49.160 --> 00:50:50.560
or I definitely don't want our friendship

00:50:50.560 --> 00:50:52.370
to be characterized by this.

00:50:52.370 --> 00:50:54.900
So for us,

00:50:54.900 --> 00:50:58.350
I mean, I only remember
going to that one seminar

00:50:59.610 --> 00:51:01.610
that was a vacation where it was like,

00:51:01.610 --> 00:51:03.260
we didn't get to do a
whole lot of vacation,

00:51:03.260 --> 00:51:06.030
because it was like I had to stand outside

00:51:06.030 --> 00:51:08.310
of these meetings that they were having.

00:51:08.310 --> 00:51:09.143
Yeah, it wasn't very fun.

00:51:09.143 --> 00:51:10.030
It was quite a downer.

00:51:10.030 --> 00:51:11.950
You didn't see much of Boca or Raton.

00:51:11.950 --> 00:51:13.690
I remember that Jimmy had an office

00:51:13.690 --> 00:51:16.590
that had a whole slew of paperwork,

00:51:16.590 --> 00:51:19.810
and then you know, stuff he was
trying to get off the ground

00:51:19.810 --> 00:51:21.160
associated with this thing.

00:51:22.502 --> 00:51:25.410
There would be some people
that he would get to like

00:51:25.410 --> 00:51:27.330
work underneath him, but I don't recall

00:51:27.330 --> 00:51:30.352
any of that really taking hold.

00:51:30.352 --> 00:51:32.380
I remember there were certain people

00:51:32.380 --> 00:51:35.200
that he seemed to idolize within the,

00:51:35.200 --> 00:51:37.500
higher-ups within the organization

00:51:37.500 --> 00:51:42.120
that were like okay, the
guy who brought me in,

00:51:42.120 --> 00:51:45.010
the guy who brought him in,
the guy who brought him in,

00:51:45.010 --> 00:51:46.750
I'm actually gonna get to meet today.

00:51:46.750 --> 00:51:50.400
And it was just like,
this weirdness, you know,

00:51:50.400 --> 00:51:53.360
that was like, how did it end for us?

00:51:53.360 --> 00:51:57.196
I don't, well I think to put it simply,

00:51:57.196 --> 00:52:00.855
they got, Mom and him got
a divorce and we moved out.

00:52:00.855 --> 00:52:02.460
That's how it ended for you.

00:52:02.460 --> 00:52:06.130
But I don't think it had anything to do

00:52:06.130 --> 00:52:09.110
with the involvement, A.L. Williams

00:52:09.110 --> 00:52:11.770
or Primerica, whatever it's called now,

00:52:11.770 --> 00:52:14.500
did not have anything
to do with their divorce

00:52:14.500 --> 00:52:16.360
or little breakup, I think
it was totally separate,

00:52:16.360 --> 00:52:18.370
and I just think it simply fizzled out.

00:52:18.370 --> 00:52:20.760
After a while of it not
working, a couple of years,

00:52:20.760 --> 00:52:23.273
it's just like this isn't working for me.

00:52:23.273 --> 00:52:24.106
You gotta buy in.

00:52:24.106 --> 00:52:24.939
What am I doing?

00:52:24.939 --> 00:52:26.770
You gotta buy in fully,
and those who do buy in

00:52:26.770 --> 00:52:29.230
can experience a lot of success.

00:52:29.230 --> 00:52:31.636
You can get that car and that house,

00:52:31.636 --> 00:52:34.470
there's just a lot that
comes along with it.

00:52:34.470 --> 00:52:36.730
So my language is a little overblown

00:52:36.730 --> 00:52:38.550
that we escaped as if it were a cult,

00:52:38.550 --> 00:52:41.278
but I do feel like it was, I mean we were,

00:52:41.278 --> 00:52:43.660
when you go into that pink hotel

00:52:43.660 --> 00:52:45.510
and you're going to all the seminars,

00:52:46.670 --> 00:52:48.970
you're bought in, we were definitely

00:52:48.970 --> 00:52:51.349
bought in for a little while.

00:52:51.349 --> 00:52:52.220
But you made it out.

00:52:52.220 --> 00:52:53.060
But we made it out.

00:52:53.060 --> 00:52:54.380
And now you know how we feel about it,

00:52:54.380 --> 00:52:56.417
but I think that the answer for @Sneakynin

00:52:56.417 --> 00:52:58.530
is you just, you have to be honest,

00:52:58.530 --> 00:52:59.917
you gotta say "I don't
want our relationship

00:52:59.917 --> 00:53:01.010
"to be characterized by this,"

00:53:01.010 --> 00:53:02.030
if you can bring yourself to that,

00:53:02.030 --> 00:53:03.330
otherwise you're just gonna sort of,

00:53:03.330 --> 00:53:05.070
the relationship will
probably just dwindle.

00:53:05.070 --> 00:53:08.340
My summary critique of it is

00:53:08.340 --> 00:53:11.827
if it's more about recruiting
people to do something

00:53:11.827 --> 00:53:16.100
that you are also doing
instead of the product itself,

00:53:16.100 --> 00:53:20.000
if you can't just isolate the
product on the open market

00:53:20.000 --> 00:53:23.740
and give it an honest
comparison and it wins,

00:53:23.740 --> 00:53:26.170
then you shouldn't believe in the product

00:53:26.170 --> 00:53:27.560
enough to pour your whole life in

00:53:27.560 --> 00:53:29.520
and change your whole lifestyle around it.

00:53:29.520 --> 00:53:31.670
I mean, it's a bunch of smoke and mirrors,

00:53:31.670 --> 00:53:34.160
it's selling a bunch of false hope.

00:53:34.160 --> 00:53:36.190
Boil it down to the product

00:53:36.190 --> 00:53:40.770
and make an honest assessment of it.

00:53:40.770 --> 00:53:43.670
Do you have to have these
vitamins and this energy drink

00:53:43.670 --> 00:53:46.150
and are you drinking their Kool-Aid?

00:53:46.150 --> 00:53:48.850
Or should you actually just
compare it to Kool-Aid?

00:53:48.850 --> 00:53:50.493
Is there a Kool-Aid one?

00:53:50.493 --> 00:53:52.690
Because I might join that one.

00:53:52.690 --> 00:53:54.150
Cool-Aid with a C,

00:53:54.150 --> 00:53:57.160
like they went uncreative.

00:53:57.160 --> 00:54:00.230
Well we took a long time
on that one, so let's--

00:54:00.230 --> 00:54:01.063
It matters.

00:54:01.063 --> 00:54:03.050
Let's answer another one here,

00:54:03.050 --> 00:54:05.970
from Rachel Daniels, which this was is,

00:54:05.970 --> 00:54:08.453
boy this hits so close to home for me.

00:54:09.290 --> 00:54:11.550
What is the best way to sign off an email?

00:54:11.550 --> 00:54:14.890
I always say "thanks" but
what about best or regards?

00:54:14.890 --> 00:54:16.660
How do I know what's appropriate?

00:54:16.660 --> 00:54:19.450
I have thought about this so thoroughly.

00:54:19.450 --> 00:54:23.470
Now, I'll just start by
giving what my practice is,

00:54:23.470 --> 00:54:26.640
and I feel stupid about
this, okay, I just feel dumb.

00:54:26.640 --> 00:54:30.530
What I do is "thanks!"
with an exclamation point,

00:54:30.530 --> 00:54:34.100
and I do it, that's
what I do almost always,

00:54:34.100 --> 00:54:37.040
even though if I'm not
thanking you for anything.

00:54:37.040 --> 00:54:38.750
Sometimes I won't do an exclamation point

00:54:38.750 --> 00:54:40.840
if I feel especially like, I'm really,

00:54:40.840 --> 00:54:42.850
there's nothing I'm thankful for at all,

00:54:42.850 --> 00:54:45.270
so I guess I'm thanking you
for reading to this point?

00:54:45.270 --> 00:54:48.130
I too, if I'm going to,

00:54:48.130 --> 00:54:49.970
sometimes I'll just sign it "Link."

00:54:49.970 --> 00:54:52.410
And then, the third
alternative is just "Rhett,"

00:54:52.410 --> 00:54:55.700
there's nothing and then I
feel like I'm being cold,

00:54:55.700 --> 00:54:58.550
I'm not being warm,
I'll do that in a reply

00:54:58.550 --> 00:55:00.620
or a chain at that point
or I might actually

00:55:00.620 --> 00:55:03.960
just drop my signature, they
know where it comes from.

00:55:03.960 --> 00:55:07.037
In one sense, it's like when my dad puts

00:55:07.037 --> 00:55:09.030
"love Dad" on a text, it's like Dad,

00:55:09.030 --> 00:55:11.740
I know where, I see that
it's coming from you.

00:55:11.740 --> 00:55:12.820
Emails work the same way,

00:55:12.820 --> 00:55:14.080
do we really have to have a signature?

00:55:14.080 --> 00:55:16.100
The from is at the top,
why put it at the bottom?

00:55:16.100 --> 00:55:18.940
Now my default is to say "thanks," comma,

00:55:18.940 --> 00:55:21.228
and then put "Link" underneath.

00:55:21.228 --> 00:55:22.301
I've probably done that.

00:55:22.301 --> 00:55:24.680
And I'm just, in terms of
what I'm thanking them for,

00:55:24.680 --> 00:55:28.199
I'm thanking them for taking the time

00:55:28.199 --> 00:55:30.510
to get that far in the
email, to actually read it.

00:55:30.510 --> 00:55:32.920
It's not about the content of the email,

00:55:32.920 --> 00:55:34.960
it's that okay, apparently
you've read it to this point.

00:55:34.960 --> 00:55:36.960
But sometimes you are
thanking them for something,

00:55:36.960 --> 00:55:38.430
so what if you are thanking
them for something?

00:55:38.430 --> 00:55:40.357
Do you say "thanks for
that," and then you're like

00:55:40.357 --> 00:55:41.780
"thanks for reading the email."

00:55:41.780 --> 00:55:42.670
Do you double up?
No.

00:55:42.670 --> 00:55:44.490
Or is it just an all-purpose thanks.

00:55:44.490 --> 00:55:45.323
No.

00:55:45.323 --> 00:55:46.156
Because that's confusing.

00:55:46.156 --> 00:55:50.607
Have you tried best or
kind regards or sincerely?

00:55:50.607 --> 00:55:54.500
Sincerely sounds like
a letter, not an email.

00:55:54.500 --> 00:55:57.990
I have friends who use best,

00:55:57.990 --> 00:56:01.940
and lots of people that
I respect use best,

00:56:01.940 --> 00:56:06.690
and I think the only
reason I can't use best

00:56:06.690 --> 00:56:09.420
is that it's such a foreign thing

00:56:09.420 --> 00:56:11.577
to the way that I think and speak,

00:56:11.577 --> 00:56:15.820
and like no one said "best" growing up.

00:56:15.820 --> 00:56:18.150
It would be, it's like
when all of a sudden,

00:56:18.150 --> 00:56:20.357
somebody says "I want you to call me Ricky

00:56:20.357 --> 00:56:23.370
